, or bevacizumab) as the gold-standard first-line treatment for center-involved DME, offering superior visual gains compared to laser photocoagulation. Focal/grid laser is typically reserved for non-center-involved DME. Corticosteroids (e.g., dexamethasone implants) are highly effective but are reserved as second-line therapy for patients who are pseudophakic or unresponsive to anti-VEGF, due to significant side effects (accelerated cataract progression and steroid-induced glaucoma/increased IOP).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-old man with a history of poorly controlled hypertension presents for a routine ophthalmic evaluation. The patient has no active visual complaints. Clinical findings include:<br>• Blood pressure: <b>186/107 mmHg</b><br>• Visual acuity: <b>20/20 in both eyes</b><br>• Dilated funduscopy: <b>Diffuse retinal arteriolar narrowing, prominent arteriovenous (AV) nicking, multiple flame-shaped hemorrhages in the nerve fiber layer, several fluffy white cotton-wool spots, and a fan-shaped pattern of yellow hard exudates radiating from the fovea (macular star)</b><br>• Optic nerve examination: <b>Pink, sharp margins, no disc edema</b><br><br>According to the Keith-Wagener-Barker classification, what is the grade of this patient''s hypertensive retinopathy, and what is the primary clinical management?', '["Grade 4 hypertensive retinopathy; immediate hospitalization for rapid intravenous antihypertensive therapy", "Grade 3 hypertensive retinopathy; gradual outpatient control of systemic blood pressure and cardiovascular risk factors", "Grade 3 hypertensive retinopathy; urgent panretinal photocoagulation (PRP)", "Grade 1 hypertensive retinopathy; observation and lifestyle modifications only", "Grade 2 hypertensive retinopathy; initiation of intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clinical findings of diffuse arteriolar narrowing and AV nicking (Grade 1 and 2 signs) *plus* **flame-shaped hemorrhages**, **cotton-wool spots** (microinfarcts of the nerve fiber layer), and **hard exudates in a macular star configuration** (Grade 3 signs), *without* optic disc swelling (Grade 4 sign), define **Grade 3 hypertensive retinopathy** under the Keith-Wagener-Barker classification system. Since visual acuity is preserved and there is no papilledema or end-organ damage indicating a hypertensive emergency, management centers on **gradual, outpatient control of systemic blood pressure** using oral antihypertensives (such as ACE inhibitors or calcium channel blocker