    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-old gentleman with a history of poorly controlled hypertension presents for a routine ophthalmic evaluation. The patient has no active visual complaints. Clinical findings include:<br>• Blood pressure: <b>185/116 mmHg</b><br>• Visual acuity: <b>20/20 in both eyes</b><br>• Dilated funduscopy: <b>Diffuse retinal arteriolar narrowing, prominent arteriovenous (AV) nicking, multiple flame-shaped hemorrhages in the nerve fiber layer, several fluffy white cotton-wool spots, and a fan-shaped pattern of yellow hard exudates radiating from the fovea (macular star)</b><br>• Optic nerve examination: <b>Pink, sharp margins, no disc edema</b><br><br>According to the Keith-Wagener-Barker classification, what is the grade of this patient''s hypertensive retinopathy, and what is the primary clinical management?', '["Grade 1 hypertensive retinopathy; observation and lifestyle modifications only", "Grade 3 hypertensive retinopathy; urgent panretinal photocoagulation (PRP)", "Grade 3 hypertensive retinopathy; gradual outpatient control of systemic blood pressure and cardiovascular risk factors", "Grade 4 hypertensive retinopathy; immediate hospitalization for rapid intravenous antihypertensive therapy", "Grade 2 hypertensive retinopathy; initiation of intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clinical findings of diffuse arteriolar narrowing and AV nicking (Grade 1 and 2 signs) *plus* **flame-shaped hemorrhages**, **cotton-wool spots** (microinfarcts of the nerve fiber layer), and **hard exudates in a macular star configuration** (Grade 3 signs), *without* optic disc swelling (Grade 4 sign), define **Grade 3 hypertensive retinopathy** under the Keith-Wagener-Barker classification system. Since visual acuity is preserved and there is no papilledema or end-organ damage indicating a hypertensive emergency, management centers on **gradual, outpatient control of systemic blood pressure** using oral antihypertensives (such as ACE inhibitors or calcium channel blockers) alongside lifestyle changes and cardiovascular risk reduction. Rapid, aggressive lowering of blood pressure is contraindicated in Grade 3 as it can cause optic nerve or cerebral hypoperfusion and stroke. No intraocular laser or injections are indicated for hypertensive retinopathy itself.'),
